        <body>
            <div xml:id="d154">

                <pb facs="0394" n="310" sameAs="frus:frus1886/pg310"/>

                <head>No. 150.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Pendleton to Mr. Bayard</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-11-16"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 16, 1885</date>.
                        (Received November 30.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 114.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose herewith an article, with
                    translation, from the Staatsburger Zeitung, of this city, of the 15th
                    instant.</p>
                <p>It has no special significance except as showing the views taken by very many
                    Germans of the power of expulsion, and its reasonable exercise in the given
                    cases.</p>
                <p>It is hardly necessary to say that the correspondent speaks for himself alone,
                    and that I am responsible for nothing except the statement of the number of
                    cases in the neighborhood of Föhr, in which my intervention was at that time
                    sought.</p>
                <p>Inclosed herewith I also transmit an article, with translation, from the Berlin
                    Preussische Zeitung of the 13th instant.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEO. H. PENDLETON.</signed></closer>



